    "reading": {
        "body": "Samsung alone accounts for more patents in this dataset than the next four organizations combined, holding 108,705 filings against a total of 311,886 across 108 companies.\n\nThe distribution is sharply concentrated at the top. Samsung and IBM together represent more than half the entire dataset. Apple, Huawei, and Microsoft occupy a middle tier, each with between 24,000 and 32,000 filings. Every organization from Oracle downward holds fewer than 8,000, and the bottom of the visible list, Palantir, sits at 1,666. That kind of power-law shape suggests that patent portfolios in this space are accumulated over decades and reflect broad technology platforms, not AI-specific strategies alone. Samsung and IBM built their counts across semiconductor, hardware, and enterprise domains long before current AI investment cycles.\n\nNVIDIA, the company most associated with the compute hardware that now constrains AI scaling, ranks tenth with 3,477 filings, well below consumer-device and enterprise-software incumbents. That gap does not mean NVIDIA has less AI-relevant IP, but the data does not distinguish AI patents from patents in adjacent domains, so the rankings reflect total portfolio size, not AI concentration specifically. The series values are stable across the most recent two weeks, with only two small increments visible, which limits what can be read about filing velocity from this snapshot alone.",
